CAT = "ieqfaContract"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("In contract law, consideration means:",
 "Something of value given by each side", ["A cooling-off period for the buyer", "The time a party takes to decide", "A written note of the agreement"],
 "Consideration is the price each party pays for the other's promise: in insurance, the premium in exchange for the insurer's promise to pay valid claims."),
("A contract is discharged by frustration when:",
 "An event makes performance impossible", ["One party regrets it", "The price of the service goes up", "One party moves to another county"],
 "Frustration ends a contract when something outside both parties' control, such as the destruction of its subject matter, makes performance impossible or radically different."),
("The usual court remedy when one party breaches a contract is:",
 "Damages for the loss caused", ["A criminal fine paid to the state", "Removal of the party's licence", "A referral to the Data Protection Commission"],
 "Damages aim to put the innocent party where they would have been had the contract been performed. Specific performance or an injunction is granted only in some cases."),
("A person may take out life cover on another person's life only where they have:",
 "An insurable interest", ["The other person's PPS number", "A joint bank account with them", "A solicitor's letter of approval"],
 "Insurable interest means the proposer would suffer a financial loss on the death of the life insured. Everyone has an unlimited insurable interest in their own life and their spouse's."),
("Under the Consumer Insurance Contracts Act 2019, before cover starts the insurer must:",
 "Ask specific questions in plain language", ["Inspect the consumer's home in person", "Obtain a medical report", "Require a guarantor for the premium"],
 "The 2019 Act puts the onus on the insurer to ask clear, specific questions about the matters it wants to know. A general question about any other relevant facts is not enough."),
("Under the Consumer Insurance Contracts Act 2019, a consumer answering a proposal form must:",
 "Answer honestly and with reasonable care", ["Volunteer every fact they can think of", "Have each answer checked by a solicitor", "Answer only the questions about their health"],
 "The consumer's duty is to answer the insurer's specific questions honestly and with reasonable care. There is no longer a general duty to volunteer facts the insurer did not ask about."),
("A consumer carelessly, but not fraudulently, gave a wrong answer on a proposal form. Under the 2019 Act, the insurer's remedy must be:",
 "In proportion to what it would have done", ["To avoid the policy from the start", "To keep all the premiums and pay nothing at all", "To report the consumer to the Gardaí"],
 "For a negligent misrepresentation the remedy is proportionate: the insurer pays the claim to the extent it would have, for example reducing it if it would have charged more."),
("Under the Consumer Insurance Contracts Act 2019, a clause that turns every proposal answer into a warranty forming the basis of the contract is:",
 "Prohibited", ["Valid if printed in bold", "Valid for life policies", "Valid with a signed waiver"],
 "Basis of contract clauses, which allowed insurers to avoid a policy for any inaccurate answer, are banned in consumer insurance contracts."),
("If a consumer made a fraudulent misrepresentation when applying, the insurer may:",
 "Avoid the policy", ["Only increase the premium", "Only reduce the claim slightly", "Do nothing at all about it"],
 "Fraud allows the insurer to avoid the contract. Proportionate remedies apply only where the consumer was careless rather than dishonest."),
("A ten-year-old sofa is destroyed in a fire. The household policy covers contents on an indemnity basis, not new for old. The insurer will pay:",
 "Its value allowing for wear and tear", ["The cost of the best new sofa", "The price paid for it ten years ago", "Nothing, as the sofa was old"],
 "Indemnity puts the insured back where they were before the loss, so a deduction is made for wear and tear. New for old cover pays the replacement cost instead."),
("Water leaking from an upstairs flat damages Ciara's kitchen. Her insurer pays her claim and then recovers the cost from the neighbour's insurer. This relies on:",
 "Subrogation", ["Contribution", "Proximate cause", "Insurable interest"],
 "Subrogation lets the insurer, once it has paid, step into the insured's shoes and pursue the party responsible for the loss."),
("Two household policies cover the same stolen bicycle. Under the principle of contribution, the insurers:",
 "Share the loss between them", ["Each pay the whole loss in full", "Pay nothing at all", "Leave it to the court"],
 "Contribution stops an insured recovering more than the loss: each insurer pays its share, so the total paid equals one indemnity."),
("A house insured for half its rebuilding cost suffers a partial fire loss. If the policy has an average clause, the claim is:",
 "Cut in proportion to the underinsurance", ["Paid in full up to the sum insured", "Refused because of the underinsurance", "Doubled to the true cost"],
 "Under average, an underinsured claim is scaled down by the ratio of the sum insured to the full value, so here about half of the loss would be paid."),
("An agent makes a contract beyond his authority, and the principal later approves it. This is called:",
 "Ratification", ["Rescission", "Frustration", "Novation"],
 "By ratifying, the principal adopts the contract as if the agent had authority from the start, so the principal is bound by it."),
("Which of these ends an agency relationship automatically?",
 "The death of the principal", ["A fall in the agent's sales", "A change in the agent's address", "A new advertising campaign"],
 "Agency ends by agreement, by completion, by notice, or automatically by events such as the death, insanity or bankruptcy of the principal or agent."),
("Under the Distance Marketing of Consumer Financial Services Regulations, a consumer who buys a life policy at a distance can withdraw within:",
 "30 days", ["7 days", "14 days", "60 days"],
 "The withdrawal period for distance contracts is normally 14 days, extended to 30 days for life assurance and personal pension contracts."),
("A consumer buys a general insurance policy entirely online. Under the distance marketing rules, the usual withdrawal period is:",
 "14 days", ["5 days", "30 days", "90 days"],
 "Most financial services contracts concluded at a distance carry a 14-day right of withdrawal without penalty or reason. Life and pension contracts get 30 days."),
("A person buys a life assurance policy in a face-to-face meeting. Irish law gives a cooling-off period, from being told the contract is concluded, of:",
 "30 days", ["7 days", "14 days", "60 days"],
 "Policyholders may cancel within 30 days of being told the contract has been concluded, and are entitled to a return of premium."),
("A motorist's insurer must deal with the claim of a person he injured on the road because Irish law requires:",
 "Third-party motor insurance", ["Health cover for drivers", "Life cover on every car loan", "A pension for every motorist"],
 "The Road Traffic Acts make insurance against liability to third parties compulsory for anyone using a mechanically propelled vehicle in a public place."),
("A policy excess is:",
 "The part of a claim the insured pays", ["The premium charged on renewal", "The insurer's profit margin on the policy", "The amount paid to the broker"],
 "The excess is the first part of each claim that the policyholder bears. A higher voluntary excess usually lowers the premium."),
]
